l wheel  and then there's your own black heart  and  all of those things can twist you  against life  and no wonder that makes you resentful  and no wonder  it's no wonder it's a miracle that you  can avoid that but it's  there's nothing about it that's helpful  you become arrogant you can't learn  you become resentful you make everything  worse you become deceitful  you you you corrupt being itself  it's so tempting and so i outline why  it's like look here's what we face  it's it's bleak it's bleak but it's not  only  we all die we have to deal with  self-conscious finitude and the  knowledge  of suffering and the and the knowledge  of injustice  and tyranny all of that and human evil  but  there's nothing good down  those pathways resentment in particular  i think resentment is unbelievably  useful  and so for practical purposes for for  everyone who's listening  if you're resentful about something well  first of all you should notice i'm  resentful and that's kind of humiliating  because  no one's proud of being resentful it's a  shameful emotion  also then why are you resentful well  there's only two reasons as far as i've  been able to tell one is that you're  you're being you're allowing yourself to  be taken advantage of or you're being  taken advantage of  sometimes you're not allowing yourself  sometimes you're a child being abused by  their uncle  you're not allowing yourself you can't  you can't do anything about it  you're resentful because you're being  taken advantage of or because  you're immature  it's one of those two things figure out  which it is and then take  the steps necessary to address it living  a life without resentment is a great  goal  it makes you you watch people drop their  resentment they get younger they get  visibly younger when they drop it it's  amazing to see that  i saw that my clinical clients all the  time they'd be resentful about something  their face would harden they'd get old  and then they'd let it go and sometimes  that was accompanied by tears and  you know you have to let it go lots just  once won't do it  but they'd let it go and then they'd  caught they'd be calm and their face  would be  unlined for for at least for a while  they'd sort of return to  a child-like state of trust or they  would re-attain that that's a better way  of thinking about it  yeah and then you can and then you can  be happy for people around you which was  one of the  one of the rules in the first book and i  i remember when i i'm sure you remember  this when we were in oslo and i walked  into the room  and i said jordan i just got a call i  got a book deal  and you were sitting in a swivel chair  and you slapped your hands together you  spun around in the chair you got up you  had a huge smile on your face  you hugged me tammy got up she had a  huge smile david came in  everybody hugged and it was like you you  were genuinely  it was one i mentioned a couple times in  the book when i saw you live up to the  rules  but it was like you were genuinely happy  there wasn't resentment like oh  now this guy got something it was this  is great well god wouldn't it be lovely  to be able to  enjoy someone else's happiness it's like  it's happiness is precious  and rare and why deny yourself that  if it's from someone else thank god they  get a break from their  catastrophe of their life and wouldn't  it be wonderful if that was happening  all the time you  you closed yourself off so badly being  resentful because everyone else's good  fortune  is equated to your misery how terrible  and that's part of the pathology of this  insistence that our culture is nothing  but a tyranny  if that's the case then anyone who  attains anything within it is only a  tyrant  and that makes everyone's  accomplishments an imposition  and theft from everyone else how awful  well you've been you've been setting it  right for a long time and  i guess that brings us perfectly to  chapter 12 which is be grateful  in spite of your suffering and i think  you probably know a little something  about that's courage  that's courage to be grateful  to remind yourself you know i say grace  with my wife every night  and i need to do that  i actually think in some ways it's the  best part of my day  strangely enough although that's not  saying much  it's very useful to remind myself to be  grateful  and the worse your situation the more  important that is  paradoxically because you can have a  terrible situation and then  also be ungrateful  be resentful and then it's worse  it's much worse  so it's courage what what if i what  what do i have to be grateful for  and to maintain that that's better for  everyone else  so that chapter is hard on me  it's sort of traumatic to read it  because i struggle with it  um  because i'm in pain most of the time  but i know that it's it's not helpful  to be resentful it's better to be  grateful  and you can do that without being naive  it's an act of will  or it's at least something to practice  so that's the book  jordan i uh  i love you man you you helped me turn my  life around because of the things that  you were courageous enough to put out  in the world and uh and i'm honored and  humbled that i was  like this much of the adventure of the  last couple years and i'm  i'm beyond thrilled we had such a good  time we did man we did like it was just  it was  just that's why it feels like a dream  because it was just  there wasn't you know john our tour  manager used to always joke you'd go  this is the most boring tour i've ever  been on no  nobody's uh wrecking hotel rooms and  nobody's  you know beating anybody up and i think  we our running joke was that the worst  thing that happened on the entire tour  was we were in calgary and i  accidentally said that we were in  edmonton which they were not happy about  but no  you were interested in toronto so  they're even worse  all right those two are at least western  canada yeah yeah yeah yeah  yeah yeah you committed a cardinal sin  for a canadian  but but truly jordan i it's just so good  to talk to you again and i i can't wait  to see you in person  and i'm i'm thrilled that you're back  nice  when we're out of this coved hell and we  and hopefully everything returns to  everything we had  when everything was normal i walked down  queen street in toronto  kind of a trendy area populated by  galleries and restaurants  and little independent shops you know  what people have poured their heart into  and they're all shuttered  every fourth business is shuttered and  you see the dreams that people  and effort that people poured into these  businesses it's so  it's heart-wrenching to see that all  taken away  i'm amazed that people i'm amazed at how  well people have done with this  i can't believe that things haven't  fallen apart more than they have  i don't know how people are managing but  maybe we'll get lucky and these  vaccines will work and we'll get what we  had back and  maybe we'll be wise enough to appreciate  it a bit more  that would be good wouldn't that be  something if we could only be  appreciative of what we had a year ago  today  before it all kind of fell apart  that would be that would be something  all right  jordan welcome back welcome back and  uh i i i good talking to you  i hope we can continue on this adventure  together and it's good to see you smile  and i'm just  i'm i'm thrilled you're back so thank  you and obviously the link to the book  is down there and all that good stuff  and and please send our love to tammy  and the whole family  if you'd like to see more honest and  insightful conversations with jordan  peterson himself check out our jordan  peterson playlist which includes  every interview i've ever done with him  and if you want to watch full interviews  on a variety of topics watch  our full episode playlist both are right  over here  and to get notified of all future videos  be sure to subscribe and click the  notification bell
 

